sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task + full intent as inputs, token minted at admission (vault.get), Gateway renders .env from the Project env template, run budget per swamp-operation-time-budgets.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten, rules 1-28 (rule 22: Ansible phases, 0600 credential file for the private fetch).
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Node work is two Ansible phases, `checkout` (clone, check out, write .env) and `setup`, behind one input step. Measured on Node 209: every placed Swamp step costs about 1.1 s of dispatch before it does any work, and the old flow ran 8 steps for the checkout and one per setup step.
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the run-folder cleanup runs beside the result step, not before it (a Swamp job runs steps by dependency depth, so they were one after the other in a create without setup or Route).
  %% status: draft
  %% command: instance:create
  %% kind: instance.create
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>reached over ssh
  CLI->>GW: instance:create<br/>PROJECT NODE NAME
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit instance:create PROJECT NODE NAME`
  %%> - syntax: `<project> <node> <name> [--root=PATH] [--domain=HOST] [--branch=BRANCH] [--database-server=SLUG]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Project, the Node, and the Instance name
  %%> - a name equal to the Project's default branch creates the `default` Instance
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Validate, reserve Instance<br/>render .env
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target Node
  %%> - the target is an active `app-dev` Node and not a task VM; a production Node is refused (`instance.candidate_required`)
  %%> - the checkout path is free, and no other change runs on the Instance; the Gateway holds one lock per Instance
  %%> Allowed:
  %%> - repeat the command for an Instance that failed or never finished: it resumes (see the failure branch)
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the Instance is reserved, with its Route when the Project serves web pages
  %%> - `.env` is rendered here from the Project's env template: `APP_URL`, a new `APP_KEY` and the database keys
  %%> - a read token for this one repository is minted, valid one hour
  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent; nothing has happened on the Node yet
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-create
  %%> Why the vault:
  %%> - the token and `.env` reach the run only through the vault; no step calls the Gateway back
  %%> Budget:
  %%> - 60 s, plus the Project's setup step timeouts, plus 30 s for the Processes, plus 180 s each for the Route and for a database copy; fixed at admission
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task ID, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  SW->>SW: Write the playbook input<br/>intent file · assets
  %%> Why one input step:
  %%> - the intent is too large for an environment variable, and the same file serves both phases
  SW->>N: Clone, write .env<br/>Ansible · tags checkout
  %%> Why one phase:
  %%> - one placed step and one Ansible start cover every task of it
  %%> Why the file:
  %%> - git reads the token from a 0600 file, so the token never enters argv; the file is removed after the fetch, success or failure
  %%> Branch:
  %%> - the requested origin branch, else the existing local branch, else a new branch from the default branch
  %%> Why 0640 orbit-env:
  %%> - the Node agent can report the key names and never the values
  %%> - `.env.testing` is written the same way, only for an owned MySQL or PostgreSQL database
  opt default Instance has a SQLite database
    SW->>N: Copy the default database<br/>child instance-clone-database
    %%> Why before setup:
    %%> - the copy lands first, so the setup steps migrate a copy, not an empty file
  end
  opt database on a Database server
    SW->>N: Create owner and databases<br/>child operation-database-create
    %%> When:
    %%> - `--database-server` is given, or the default Instance's database is MySQL on a server; that dump is then copied in
  end
  SW->>N: Run Project setup steps<br/>Ansible · tags setup
  %%> As whom:
  %%> - the steps run as the Node's configured user, one after another; the first failure stops the run
  %%> When:
  %%> - a Project without setup steps runs no setup phase
  SW->>N: Create, start Processes<br/>child operation-process-create
  %%> Why after setup:
  %%> - Processes need the installed dependencies
  opt web-serving Project
    SW->>N: Create the Route<br/>child operation-route-create
    %%> What it adds:
    %%> - the same steps as `route:create`, then a TLS probe of the site
    %%> Why it marks the Instance awake:
    %%> - so the first request reaches the app, not the wake page; only when this run starts every Process
  end
  alt all steps pass in budget
    N-->>SW: Site answers<br/>Processes running
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record result
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Instance and its Route become active
    %%> - a successful create means every setup step succeeded; an empty list counts as success
  else Instance already created
    Note over GW: Skipped — already created<br/>instance.exists
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Instance is active and its first setup finished, so no step runs
    %%> - the skip reason is part of the successful result in human and machine output, not an error
  else a step fails or the budget ends
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or timeout
    %%> When:
    %%> - a command exits non-zero, a Process or the site does not come up, or the budget ends
    SW->>N: Remove what the run made<br/>same steps as instance:destroy
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - withdraws the Route, runs the Project teardown steps, then removes the Processes, the database the run created and the checkout
    %%> - removes only what this run made; other Instances, the Database server and the default Instance's database stay
    %%> Why remove:
    %%> - the failed step and its output are in the task, so nothing on the Node is needed to see what failed
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step, error_code
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its error code; a missing setup command is `instance.setup_step_unavailable`, any other setup failure `instance.setup_step_failed`
    %%> - the original error code is kept, whether or not cleanup finished
    Note over GW: Record the failure
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the task fails with the failed step; when cleanup finished, the Instance and its Route no longer exist
    %%> If cleanup did not finish:
    %%> - the Instance stays marked failed with `cleanup = incomplete`; the incomplete cleanup stays visible, and a crash is never taken as proof that cleanup ran
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same command again: it finishes an incomplete cleanup first, then creates; a changed `--root` or `--branch` is refused until then (`instance.placement_conflict`)
    %%> - `instance:destroy` also finishes the cleanup
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
